    {
      "text": "Whenever three or more of these get together, they sing in a loud voice that sounds like bellowing.",
      "version": "x"
    },
    {
      "text": "If Poliwag and Poliwhirl hear its echoing cry, they respond by gathering from far and wide.",
      "version": "y"
    },
    {
      "text": "The curled hair on Politoed’s head is proof of its status as a king. It is said that the longer and more curled the hair, the more respect this Pokémon earns from its peers.",
      "version": "omega-ruby"
    },
    {
      "text": "The curled hair on Politoed’s head is proof of its status as a king. It is said that the longer and more curled the hair, the more respect this Pokémon earns from its peers.",
      "version": "alpha-sapphire"
    },
    {
      "text": "They gather on moonlit nights to form a large chorus. Their cries sound angry and not at all pleasant, but they are certainly distinctive.",
      "version": "sun"
    },
    {
      "text": "It’s the leader of Poliwag and Poliwhirl. When Politoed roars, they all cower in fear.",
      "version": "moon"
    },
    {
      "text": "Although its cries sound like screams, a composer created a beautiful ballad that was influenced by the sounds.",
      "version": "ultra-sun"
    },
    {
      "text": "The longer and more luxurious the curled hair on its head, the greater the number of Poliwag and Poliwhirl that will obey it.",
      "version": "ultra-moon"
    },
    {
      "text": "At nightfall, these Pokémon appear on the shores of lakes. They announce their territorial claims by letting out cries that sound like shouting.",
      "version": "sword"
    },
    {
      "text": "The cry of a male is louder than that of a female. Male Politoed with deep, menacing voices find more popularity with the opposite gender.",
      "version": "shield"
    }
  ],
